  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1159 AM MST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nMoving into mid-week, a quick-moving upper trough will pass through \nour region, starting Tuesday Night and lasting into early Wednesday. \nShowers will start over the San Juans, spreading to the rest of the \nhigher terrain and parts of the San Luis Valley overnight. The \nsystem has trended faster over the last few sets of model runs, and \na bit more north, meaning that impacts have generally decreased. As \nof now, latest snow totals show localized 3-6 inch accumulations \nover the San Juans, and lighter amounts (generally an inch or less) \nover the rest of the mountain peaks. The San Luis Valley should see \nmostly rain, and currently little to no snow accumulation is \nexpected. Snow levels should remain above 7kft at the lowest until \nearly Wednesday morning, by which POPs already start dropping off as \nthe trough pushes east of us.\n\nWe'll see a lull in active weather on Thursday, with dry and mild \nconditions for SE Colorado. Highs will be in the 50s-60s again. \nThursday Night and into the weekend, another, stronger system will \nbegin moving in from the northwest. Latest model guidance still \nshows some inconsistency, though the trends suggest a broad upper \ntrough carrying two separate portions of energy that will impact our \nregion. POPs will pick up over the SW mountains again starting later \nThursday Night, with more active weather lasting into early \nSaturday, though that could change depending on the evolution of the \nsystem. The system has also been trending more north, meaning that \nprecipitation estimates have once again decreased for our CWA. While \nthe San Juans will likely see some more accumulating snow, the rest \nof the area remains in flux in terms of QPF and related impacts. \n\nBeyond this weekend, another broad trough appears to be on deck for \nearly next week, though it is still far too early for details to \nbecome clear.",
